#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119543 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33586620-e110-5a37-874f-a92103c5e9fb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for One Coefficient in a Linear Combination"}] :question/id #uuid "fd3cb5b1-f16e-5a52-82e4-25bbbd1c4fe7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4789" :question/problem "Find $s$, if $3\\mathbf{a_1}+s\\mathbf{a_2} = \\mathbf{b}$ and\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $s$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3a_{1} + sa_{2} &= b \\\\\n3\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + s\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\ns\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} - 3\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\ns\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the final equation above, we can tell that $s=4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119544 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "590953b8-2533-5a4b-bf4f-f002b0367919" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Vector in a Linear Combination"}] :question/id #uuid "548ef945-392b-5e75-9b60-14f4e2de6261" :question/math-academy-id "e-4790" :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{x}$, if $\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}$ and\n$\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate $\\mathbf{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na + 2x &= b \\\\\n2x &= b - a \\\\\nx &= \\frac{1}{2}(b - a) \\\\\nx &= \\frac{1}{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}24 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\nx &= \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nx &= \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119545 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92e5e826-3121-500a-9103-e618cb16c5b2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Two Coefficients in a Linear Combination"}] :question/id #uuid "13ebde04-8518-5837-9420-c9487debc562" :question/math-academy-id "e-4791" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIf $x_1 \\mathbf{c}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{c}_2 = \\mathbf d$, then what is $x_1+x_2$?" :question/worked-solution "If\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{c}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{c}_2 = \\mathbf d\n$$\n\nthen we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} + x_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}4x_{1} + x_{2} \\\\ -x_{1} \\\\ 3x_{1} + x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{4x_{1} + x_{2} = 5; - x_{1} =-5; 3x_{1} + x_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we obtain $x_1=5$, which we substitute into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x_{1} + x_{2} &= 5 \\\\\n4(5) + x_{2} &= 5 \\\\\nx_{2} &= -15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x_1=5$ and $x_2=-15$ into the third equation to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x_{1} + x_{2} &= 0 \\\\\n3(5) + (-15) &= 0 \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a true statement.\n\nSo the solution of the system is $x_1=5$ and $x_2=-15$, which means\n\n$$\n5 \\mathbf{c_1}-15 \\mathbf{c_2} = \\mathbf{d}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nx_1+x_2=5-15=-10\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119593 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13a44dc8-4699-519f-8cb1-87ad77471ebe" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Three Coefficients in a Linear Combination"}] :question/id #uuid "d9beff9d-db58-540f-811e-a07fb77d3d92" :question/math-academy-id "e-6191" :question/problem "Consider the vectors\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIf $x_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{a}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{a}_3 = \\mathbf b$, then what is $x_1+x_2+x_3$?" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{b}$ is a linear combination of vectors $\\mathbf{a_1}, \\,\\mathbf{a_2}$ and $\\mathbf{a_3}$ with coefficients $x_1,\\, x_2$ and $x_3$, respectively, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}a_{1} + x_{2}a_{2} + x_{3}a_{3} &= b \\\\\nx_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + x_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} + x_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ x_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}x_{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}x_{3} \\\\ 2x_{3} \\\\ 3x_{3}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{2} + x_{3} \\\\ x_{1} + 2x_{3} \\\\ -x_{2} + 3x_{3}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{x_{2} + x_{3} = 1; x_{1} + 2x_{3} = 1; - x_{2} + 3x_{3} = 7\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{1} & ↔R_{2} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} + R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{3} \\mid:=\\frac{1}{4}R_{3} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n{x_{1} + 2x_{3} = 1; x_{2} + x_{3} = 1; x_{3} = 2\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $x_3 = 2$. Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2+(2) = 1 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x_2 = -1\n$$\n\nand substituting $x_3=2$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1+2(2) = 1 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x_1 = -3\n$$\n\nSo the solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx_1=-3, x_2=-1, x_3=2\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\n-3 \\mathbf{a_1}- \\mathbf{a_2} +2 \\mathbf{a_3}= \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nx_1+x_2+x_3=-3-1+2=-2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120243 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "590953b8-2533-5a4b-bf4f-f002b0367919" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Vector in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "972a3fef-203d-572e-9899-5e0b2c6e0630" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117959} {:answer/id #uuid "a83599ad-2be5-5e43-b83a-069c19db8cb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117960} {:answer/id #uuid "f2b2e220-aa44-53a1-8e91-1098cf158db8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117961} {:answer/id #uuid "3896fd9b-3724-5e00-a5e0-7c3c15a1e476" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117962} {:answer/id #uuid "38cca5f0-0e27-56f5-a922-3d986adc2f8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a83599ad-2be5-5e43-b83a-069c19db8cb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 1